            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of plasma serotonin concentration in acute appendicitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Due to lack of reliable biochemical/radiological markers, the diagnosis of acute appendicitis is based only on clinical features.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We estimated plasma serotonin levels in 48 patients with acute appendicitis (histologically proven), 27 patients with abdominal pain of other etiologies, and 20 healthy controls.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The plasma serotonin levels were (mean +/- SD) 36.6 +/- 12.5 nmol/L, 12.5 +/- 3.6 nmol/L and 10.4 +/- 3.5 nmol/L in the three groups, respectively. The levels in patients with acute appendicitis were significantly higher (p &lt; 0.001) than in the other groups, giving 93.8% sensitivity and 95.7% specificity to the test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Plasma serotonin level is a reliable marker of acute appendicitis, especially in the first 48 hours.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
